[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Cooklang turns your recipes into plain text files you own forever and ships a one binary CLI that builds your shopping lists, plans your week, and serves this web UI to every device in your house. It\'s markdown for your kitchen.'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Ingredients, cookware, and timers marked up inline, parsable by anything, locked into nothing. This project has been quietly compounding for 5 years, and it\'s one of the most charming ideas in open source'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'right now. Let\'s cook. This is cooklang.org, and the pitch takes one sentence. Recipes are data, so store them like data. Every recipe app you\'ve ever used holds your recipes hostage. An account, a subscription, a database you'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can\'t export cleanly, a company that can shut down. Cooklang\'s answer is a text file per recipe, readable by humans, parsable by computers, versioned in Git if you want. No account, works offline, open source.'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And stick around for the menu file trick near the end. It plans an entire week and hands you one shopping list. Here\'s an actual Cooklang file, and you can read it like a recipe because it is one. Ingredients get an at sign with'

[01:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'quantities in braces. At semolina, at mozzarella cheese, 100 g. Cookware gets a hash. The outdoor oven, the spatula. Timers get a tilde. 90 seconds for a Neapolitan bake. Everything else is just'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prose. That\'s the entire language. A parser pulls out the structure, every ingredient, every quantity, every tool, every timer, while the file stays perfectly readable on paper, in a text editor, or 20 years from now. The'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Cooklang organization on GitHub describes the mission in one line. A lightweight, human-readable language designed to make cooking data portable, programmable, and future-proof. 34 public repositories sit under that'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'banner. The spec, the Rust CLI, mobile apps, parsers in multiple languages, editor plugins. This isn\'t a hype project riding a trend cycle. It\'s 5 years of steady, patient ecosystem'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'building, and that\'s exactly what makes it feel durable. The design principles deserve a moment because they\'re the opposite of how consumer software gets built now. Your recipes live in files you own forever. No cloud dependency, no'

[02:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'export button to hunt for because there\'s nothing to export from. Everything works offline. There\'s no account anywhere in the ecosystem. And because it\'s a spec, not a product, anyone can build a parser, which is why'

[02:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'there are community implementations across languages and plugins for VS Code, Vim, Emacs, and Sublime. The format outlives any single tool built on it. If Markdown taught us anything, it\'s'

[02:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that a simple text convention can quietly become infrastructure. Cooklang is making the same bet for the kitchen. The site is a pleasure on a phone, and that matters because the kitchen is a phone environment. The ecosystem meets'

[02:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you there, too. Native iOS and Android apps will sync for actually cooking from your files. Hands on the counter, timers tapping into the markup. The free cook editor desktop app adds syntax highlighting and live preview for'

[02:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'writing. And import tools convert recipes from hundreds of websites into Cooklang, so you\'re not retyping your collection to get started. Cook CLI is the power tool, a recipe manager and a web server compiled into one Rust'

[03:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'binary. Brew install cook cli, then cook c drop sample recipes to play with. Cook recipe pretty prints any file, ingredients totaled, cookware listed, steps numbered. Cook shopping list merges multiple recipes into one'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'organized list. And this is where the Unix philosophy pays off. It\'s all standard output, so pipe it into whatever your home automation speaks. Then cook server turns the same binary into a website serving your collection'

[03:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to every device on your network. Notice the output when it starts. It\'s just pointing at a folder of dot cook files. The server is a view. The plain text remains the source of truth. And here\'s that server running. This is'

[03:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'demo.cooklang.org, the actual embedded web UI, browsable right now, recipe cards with images, structured ingredients, step-by-step mode, the shopping list view, even pantry tracking with expiration dates.'

[03:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Remember, this entire interface ships inside the CLI binary. No Docker Compose, no database, no Node modules. One file on a Raspberry Pi serves your household\'s cookbook. They publish ARM'

[04:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'builds precisely for that. Now, the payoff I promised, menu files. A dot menu file plans your week. Saturday breakfast references your oats with quantities in line. Dinner points at the pizza recipe file. Sunday pulls'

[04:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the pancakes and CookCLI generates one combined shopping list for the entire plan, scaled to your servings. Add custom reports for evaluating recipes or menus, pantry tracking so the list skips what'

[04:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you already have, and recipe search across the collection. Scriptable meal planning in plain text with your own files. It\'s the kind of feature that sounds small until Sunday afternoon, when the week\'s list writes itself, and you'

[04:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'realize the format is doing work a subscription app charges for. Full transparency on scale. CookCLI sits at 1,343 stars, the spec at 688, and the org counts 34 repositories over 5 years'

[05:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of development. Those are modest numbers next to the 100,000 star monsters we usually cover, and that\'s fine. This is the other kind of open source. No venture round, no trend cycle, just a'

[05:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'spec and a toolchain getting a little better every release. 32 versions in. Projects like this tend to outlast the hype-driven ones because the format is the product. Put it next to the recipe app model, and the trade is stark. Apps'

[05:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'give you polish up front and take ownership in exchange. Your collection lives in their database, behind their account, at the mercy of their subscription pricing and their runway. When the app sunsets, and they do, you'

[05:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'get a JSON export at best. Cookling inverts every one of those. Text files on your disk, any editor, any parser, get history if you\'re that person, and a format that will open in 2050 exactly the way it opens today. The'

[05:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'polish gap is real. Apps are prettier on day one, but ownership compounds, and pretty doesn\'t. The site makes the promise in nine words. Keep your recipes in files you own forever. No account'

[06:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'required, works offline, open source. It reads like a manifesto for a whole category of software we quietly lost. Tools that respect that your data might matter longer than their company does.'

[06:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Grandma\'s recipes deserve a file format, not a subscription. The repo card, cooklang/cookcli, rust, MIT license, 1,343 stars, 97 forks, version 0.32.1'

[06:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shipped in June. It installs with brew, cargo, or a pre-built binary for every platform including arm Linux. The code base is a genuinely nice read if you\'re learning rust CLI design.'

[06:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'One binary doing argument parsing, recipe parsing, report generation, and an embedded web server without ceremony. That\'s cooklang, a small project with a big patient idea, and one we\'re'

[06:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'genuinely excited to watch grow. Write one recipe in it this week, and subscribe to Prism Labs for more open source gems.'
